<BaseLayout title="About the project" description="Read the mission, editorial principles, international translation plans, and public participation goals behind Whaling Watch.">
  <section class="container closing-section about-page">
    <p class="eyebrow">About Whaling Watch</p>
    <h1>Document the evidence.<br />Preserve the context.</h1>
    <p>Whaling Watch is an independent, international information project about whales, whaling, strandings, historical events, regulation, and related science. We document the subject without treating a country, community, incident, or individual claim as shorthand for the whole picture.</p>
    <p>Our aim is simple: make reliable context easier to reach—across commercial activity, Aboriginal and subsistence whaling, law, science, culture, logistics, history, and the public record.</p>
    <div class="mission-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">The mission</p>
      <h2>A reference that outlasts any one industry.</h2>
      <p>Our mission is to document and preserve the global history, economics, practices and governance of whaling through accessible information with traceable sources.</p>
      <p>Whaling Watch does not advocate for the continuation or cessation of whaling. We provide historical context, document contemporary developments and help readers find reliable records. Whether whaling continues, changes or ceases entirely, its history remains worth understanding.</p>
      <p>This is a growing reference, not a claim to contain everything. Our <a href="/historical-economics/">historical economics guide</a> and <a href="/countries/history/">country-history inventory</a> make the current evidence and its limits visible.</p>
    </div>
    <section class="about-principles">
      <h2>Consistent standards. Room for correction.</h2>
      <p>We distinguish documented events from attributed explanations, proposals from enacted law, and commercial activity from Indigenous and subsistence practices. Neutrality means applying consistent standards of evidence, including when the evidence describes harm or disagreement.</p>
      <p>People and organizations directly involved should have a fair opportunity to explain their positions. We welcome corrections and primary-source material. Listening does not promise agreement or publication: claims still require appropriate attribution and verification.</p>
      <p>Sources, gaps and corrections belong in view. Read our <a href="/sources/">source standards</a> and <a href="/blog/">corrections and changes</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="about-principles" id="languages">
      <h2>International history, international access.</h2>
      <p>Short introductions are now available in Icelandic and Japanese through the language links in the footer. Most of the site is still in English. Our long-term goal is for every page to be available in the languages needed by our readers and the communities we cover, including Icelandic and Japanese.</p>
      <p>Full-page translations will follow language and factual review; legal wording must be checked against the original sources. <a href="/contact/">Translation and review contributions are welcome.</a></p>
    </section>
    <div class="participate-panel">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">A public watch</p><h2>Help us see what we missed.</h2></div>
      <div><p>Researchers, coastal communities, photographers, translators, journalists, and careful readers are invited to share sources, context, corrections, and original material. Participation does not require agreement—only good faith and a willingness to show the evidence.</p><a class="button button-primary" href="/contact/">Contribute to the project →</a></div>
    </div>
    <div class="artist-credit">
      <p class="eyebrow">Artwork</p>
      <h2>Angga gave Whaling Watch its inhabitants.</h2>
      <p>Angga’s original whale illustrations bring warmth, curiosity, and a wonderfully strange personality to a subject that is too often presented without either. Their work helps this project feel inviting without weakening its seriousness.</p>
      <p>If you enjoy the whales swimming through this site, please <a href="https://vgen.co/AnggaPokoke/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">visit Angga on VGen</a>, follow their work, or consider supporting them with a commission of your own.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
